2. Types of Methods in Java (Big Picture)
By Implementation:
Concrete methods
Abstract methods
Default methods (interface)
Static methods

By Modifiers / Behavior:
final
synchronized
native
private, protected, public

By Purpose:
Instance methods
Utility methods
Factory methods
Lifecycle callbacks

3. Concrete Method (Most Common)
What is a Concrete Method?

Has method body
Can be instance or static
Can be overridden (unless final)

Example
class Calculator {
    int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }
}

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Calculator c = new Calculator();
        System.out.println(c.add(10, 20));
    }
}

Output
30

4. Static Method
What is a Static Method?

Belongs to the class
Can be called without object
Cannot use this or super
Cannot be overridden (method hiding)

Example
class MathUtil {
    static int square(int x) {
        return x * x;
    }
}

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(MathUtil.square(5));
    }
}

Output
25

Interview Rules

Accessed using class name
Compile-time binding
Used for utility logic

5. Instance Method

Belongs to an object
Can access instance variables
Supports runtime polymorphism

class Person {
    String name = "Sai";

    void greet() {
        System.out.println("Hello " + name);
    }
}

Output
Hello Sai

6. Abstract Method
What is an Abstract Method?
Declared with abstract
No body
Must be implemented by subclass
Used to define contract

Example
abstract class Vehicle {
    abstract void start();
}

class Car extends Vehicle {
    void start() {
        System.out.println("Car started");
    }
}

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Vehicle v = new Car();
        v.start();
    }
}

Output
Car started

Rules
Abstract method ❌ cannot be final, static, or private
Enables runtime polymorphism

7. Default Method (Interface – Java 8+)
What is a Default Method?
Interface method with implementation
Helps in backward compatibility

Example
interface Logger {
    default void log() {
        System.out.println("Logging...");
    }
}

class AppLogger implements Logger {}

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new AppLogger().log();
    }
}

Output
Logging...

Key Points
Can be overridden
Resolved at runtime

8. Static Method in Interface
interface Util {
    static int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }
}

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(Util.add(10, 20));
    }
}

Output
30

✔ Called using interface name only

9. Final Method
What is a Final Method?
Cannot be overridden
Used to lock behavior

class Parent {
    final void show() {
        System.out.println("Final method");
    }
}

class Child extends Parent {
    // void show() {} ❌
}

Output
Final method

10. Synchronized Method (Thread Safety)
What is a Synchronized Method?
Allows only one thread at a time
Acquires object or class lock

class Counter {
    int count = 0;

    synchronized void increment() {
        count++;
    }
}

✔ Prevents race condition
❌ Performance cost

11. Native Method
What is a Native Method?

Implemented in C/C++
Uses JNI

class NativeDemo {
    native void show();
}

✔ Used for OS-level operations
❌ Rare in application code

12. Private Method

Accessible only within the class
Cannot be overridden
Can be overloaded

class Test {
    private void secret() {
        System.out.println("Private method");
    }

    void call() {
        secret();
    }
}

Output
Private method

13. Protected Method

Accessible in:
Same package
Subclasses (even in different package)

class Parent {
    protected void show() {
        System.out.println("Protected method");
    }
}

14. Method Overloading (Compile-Time Polymorphism)
class Calculator {
    int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }
    double add(double a, double b) { return a + b; }
}

Output
30
30.5

✔ Same name, different parameters
❌ Return type alone not allowed

15. Method Overriding (Runtime Polymorphism)
class Parent {
    void show() {
        System.out.println("Parent");
    }
}

class Child extends Parent {
    void show() {
        System.out.println("Child");
    }
}

public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Parent p = new Child();
        p.show();
    }
}

Output
Child

16. Static vs Instance vs Abstract (🔥 Important Table)
| Feature       | Static | Instance | Abstract |
| ------------- | ------ | -------- | -------- |
| Belongs to    | Class  | Object   | Contract |
| Override      | ❌      | ✅        | Must     |
| Polymorphism  | ❌      | ✅        | Enables  |
| Access `this` | ❌      | ✅        | ❌        |
